  "error_map": {
    "400:invalid_grant": "auth_revoked",
    "400:invalid_scope": "permission_denied",
    "400:unauthorized_client": "permission_denied",
    "400:invalid_request": "invalid_request",
    "401:invalid_client": "auth_revoked",
    "401:unauthorized": "auth_revoked",
    "401:invalid_token": "auth_expired",
    "403:insufficient_scope": "permission_denied"
  },
  "_error_map_note": "Reference: RFC 6749 section 5.2 (token-endpoint error codes) and RFC 6750 section 3.1 (Bearer resource errors) -- the vocabulary the IAS token endpoint at https://api.integralplatform.com/uaa/oauth/token actually emits, since it is a Cloud Foundry UAA server and UAA answers the standard OAuth error set verbatim. IAS publishes no numbered, provider-specific error table (re-checked 2026-08-17: helpcenter.integralplatform.com/article/reporting-api documents statuses only), so the OAuth vocabulary is the only enumerated code set this connector can key on, and every key here is one of the RFC's finite values -- none is invented. PAYLOAD SHAPE: the body is {\"error\":\"invalid_token\",\"error_description\":\"Access token expired: ...\"}, i.e. a STRING at the top-level 'error' key -- a shape core.pull_errors._extract_provider_codes does not read (it only descends into an 'error' OBJECT). connector._ias_error_payload() therefore copies that string into the top-level 'reason' slot, which core consults first, keeping every original key as evidence (the stripe/square normalisation pattern; core stays free of provider vocabulary). WHAT THE KEYS BUY: '400:invalid_grant' and '401:unauthorized' ('Bad credentials' from the UAA password grant) become auth_revoked instead of invalid_request / auth_expired -- the resource-owner credential IAS issued no longer exists, so the repair is a new credential rather than a refresh or a corrected request; '400:invalid_scope' and '400:unauthorized_client' become permission_denied, i.e. ask for the reporting scope, not fix the request. 429 stays on the core.quota.RateLimitError breaker path and is never keyed here.",
